INT. FENCING SALLE - BASEMENT OF AN OLD OPERA HOUSE - NIGHT

Chandelier dust, mirrored walls, a piste of grey tape on parquet. One bare bulb.

ASTRID NORDQUIST (30s, veteran, scarred forearm) fences a padded dummy with grim precision. Lunge, recover, lunge. Her reflection lunges with her. Sweat darkens her whites. A cracked wall clock reads midnight.

She stops. Lowers the blade. Studies her wrist, which trembles, and presses it flat against her thigh until it quiets.

On the stair, a door opens. Footsteps. A young man in a hooded coat descends, glove bag over one shoulder. This is OLIVER BANGURA (20s, upstart, shoulders too square). He stops at the foot of the stairs.

OLIVER
They said you'd be here.

ASTRID
They said wrong.

OLIVER
You're holding a sword.

She flicks the blade at the spare rack. He unzips his bag, pulls out a mask, and takes a foil. They salute. The blades touch.

She wins the first touch cleanly. He loses the second to a feint he should have read. The third is his: a low riposte she cannot answer. She laughs once, short, and shakes out her wrist.

ASTRID
Who taught you that?

OLIVER
You did.

ASTRID
I taught you nothing.

OLIVER
You taught me by losing to me.

She stares at him. Then she takes off her mask, throws it across the room, and the mirrors shiver. He doesn't flinch. She steps in close.

ASTRID
Again.
